    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "$\\int 2{x}^{7}{({x}^{8}+15)}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the $2{x}^{7}$ that's outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $({x}^{8}+15).$ But the derivative is $8{x}^{7},$ so we're only out by a constant factor. \n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{8}+15.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =8{x}^{7}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u={x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{x}^{7}{({x}^{8}+15)}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{({x}^{8}+15)}^{7}\\cdot {x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2{u}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{u}^{8}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{({x}^{8}+15)}^{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
