    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int x({x}^{2}+29)^{10}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the $x$ that's outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $({x}^{2}+29).$ But the derivative is $2x,$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+29.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x({x}^{2}+29)^{10}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}+29)^{10}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{10}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{10}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{11}}{11}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}{u}^{11}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}({x}^{2}+29)^{11}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+29)^{11}}{22}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
